    40	## 2. The canonicalization operator (the treatment)
    41	
    42	### 2.1 Definition (per sample, on the metadata dict produced by `AR_md.py`)
    43	
    44	Inputs: `depth` `[3, H, W]` float64 camera-frame point cloud (`convert_equirect_to_camera_coord`, listener at the origin), poses `source [3]`, `source_vit [1,3]`, `context_poses [N,3]`, `context_poses_vit [N,3]`; `W = 512`, `H = 256`. Fail-closed input contract (review H8): `depth.ndim == 3`, `depth.shape[0] == 3`, **H even** (odd H puts a row at `φ = 0` ⇒ zero denominator), finite, floating dtype; poses present with the exact shapes above, finite, floating, `N ≥ 1`.
    45	
    46	**Step A — bitwise yaw-stable recovered-depth proxy** (renamed per review H4; not an exact inverse of the raw `.npy`). `D[h, w] = depth[2, h, w] / (−sin φ_h)`, `φ_h = (h + 0.5)·π/H − π/2` built exactly as in `AR_md.py` (`torch.arange(H)` → float32 angles → `torch.sin`), cast to `depth.dtype` at the division; the code asserts `min_h |sin φ_h| > 0`. The reviewed yaw operator `rotate_scene_metadata` rolls columns and rotates the vectors with a float32-built matrix: the z channel is bitwise unchanged by a rotation about z (matrix row `[0,0,1]`) and `sin φ_h` is a per-row constant, so `D` after un-rolling equals the unrotated `D` **bitwise** (probe: 0/300 mismatches), whereas the per-pixel L2 norm does not (300/300). `D` agrees with the raw `.npy` to 8.9e-16 (91 % of pixels bitwise) — adequate for the "deepest pixel" semantics, and the full audit (§7 rung 3) checks raw-vs-proxy agreement of the *candidate set and the selected column* on all 9,650 maps.
    47	
    48	**Step B — candidates.** `P[w] = max_h D[h, w]`, `M = max_w P[w]`, `C = {w : P[w] == M}` (exact equality on the bitwise-stable proxy).
    49	
    50	**Step C — equivariant tie-break (D1 = Option B, Yixun 2026-10-23; strengthened per review B1).** `|C| = 1` → `j* = C[0]`. Otherwise compare candidates by a lexicographic key that is a *rotation-invariant object read from the candidate anchor*, in this order:
    51	1. **the full rooted image** `(D[:, c], D[:, c+1 mod W], …, D[:, c+W−1 mod W])` (each column an H-vector, top to bottom; exact float comparison) — largest wins. Two candidates tie here only if the depth image is exactly periodic with period `(c₂ − c₁) mod W` (the column-max profile alone was not enough — review B1).
    52	2. **the relative azimuth of the target source** `((atan2(y_s, x_s) − θ_c) mod 2π)`, `θ_c = (c + 0.5)·2π/W − π` — smallest wins; skipped if `r_s < 1e-6` (azimuth undefined). The wrap is taken on the *difference* so a source at the ±π boundary is handled by the modulo, not by a comparison of raw angles (tested across all 512 rolls).
    53	3. **the sorted multiset of relative context azimuths** (same rule per context row, sorted ascending; degenerate rows mapped to `+inf` and sorted last) — lexicographically smallest wins.
    54	4. If still tied, the **joint input (depth image, source, context poses) has a nontrivial stabilizer** `g` ≠ identity: every surviving candidate maps the joint input to the *same* canonical input (identical up to float32 rotation-composition round-off), so the model output cannot depend on the choice. The code picks `min(C)` and flags the sample as `tie_class = "stabilized"` — **this is not a residual non-equivariance** (review B1 asked for fail-closed; the Planner's position is that aborting a 43 h training on an exactly symmetric synthetic room + symmetric sources is unwarranted because the model input is provably identical; the count is logged in training (W&B scalar + end-of-fit print) and recorded per cell at eval, and the §7 audit over every `(panorama, source, contexts)` triple of the train/unseen/seen splits reports the expected count, which is 0 unless the rooms are exactly symmetric). *Sub-decision A2 in §11.*
    55	`tie_class ∈ {none, same_column, cross_column_image, cross_column_source, cross_column_context, stabilized}` is returned with `j*`.
    56	*Option A (literal argmax-first; rejected by Yixun, kept as the negative control test):* non-equivariant on the 3.75 % cross-column-tie panoramas (frame jumps 0.7°–80°) — an xfail-strict test documents it.
    57	
    58	**Step D — the shift.** `dj = (W − j*) mod W`, `α = dj·2π/W`, `md_canon = rotate_scene_metadata(md, α, W)` — the reviewed exp_02 operator (`torch.roll(depth, dj, dims=2)` puts column `j*` at column 0 — AR columns increase with azimuth, positive `α` rolls by `+dj` and applies `R_z(+α)` [verified by the reviewer]; depth vectors and all four pose fields rotated by `R_z(α)`; `context_audio`, `padding_mask`, `scene`, and the target RIR untouched — a rigid yaw leaves the RIR unchanged). **Post-condition asserted, fail-closed:** recomputing Steps A–C on `md_canon` yields `j* = 0` and the same `tie_class`.
    59	
    60	**Exactness statement.** For any integer roll `d`, `canon(rot_d(md))` and `canon(md)` have the *identical recovered-depth image* `D_canon` (bitwise; the hash of §5's Tier 1) and vector/pose values equal up to the composition `R(dj')·R(d)` vs `R(dj)` of float32 rotation matrices (≤ ~1e-6 relative). bf16 conditioning autocast then rounds most of that away; the residual is *measured* (`ε_cond`, Tier 2), never assumed. (A bitwise variant — rebuild the point cloud from `D` — is possible for `depth` but not for the poses and is NOT proposed.) Naming (review N10): in tie cases 2–3 this is a *joint geometry-plus-query canonicalizer*; the record carries the tie class so those items are identifiable. Cost (review N11): one `[3,256,512]` division/max/argmax + one roll/rotation per sample; measured latency is reported from the smoke run and the eval logs, not asserted as zero.
    61	
    62	Idempotence: a second application computes `dj = 0` and returns tensors `torch.equal` to its input.
    63	
    64	### 2.2 Where it is applied
    65	
    66	- **Training wrapper** (`src/training/diffusion.py`): constructor kwargs `canonicalize_enabled: bool = False`, `canonicalize_img_w: int = 512`, validated on the RAW arguments exactly like `yaw_aug_*`. Applied as the **first** metadata transform in `training_step`, `validation_step` AND `test_step` via one method `_canonicalize_batch(metadata)`: (i) the same fail-closed schema checks as `_check_yaw_aug_metadata` plus §2.1's contract, prefix `canonicalize:`; (ii) `canonicalize_scene_metadata` per sample; (iii) accumulates `tie_class` counts (`self.canon_tie_counts`), logs `canon/stabilized_ties` and `canon/cross_column_ties` cumulatively (rank-0, every `log_every_n_steps`) and prints the totals at `on_fit_end`. **Mutual exclusion (construction + factory):** with `yaw_aug_enabled` → `ValueError`; with `cond_method == 'fa_invariant'` → `ValueError`. Rank-0 banner on `on_fit_start`: **`canonicalize ENABLED img_w=512 tiebreak=image+src+ctx`**.
    67	- **Factory** (`src/training/factory.py`): `_parse_canonicalize_config(training_config)` mirroring `_parse_yaw_aug_config` (keys exactly `{enabled, img_w}`; absent block or `enabled: false` → `{}` ⇒ the disabled path is the pre-change call verbatim; exp_15's golden regression `test_yaw_aug_training.py` must stay green).
    68	- **Inference** (`eval_FLAC.py`):
    69	  - `--canonicalize` (store_true), applied **after** `apply_rotation_plan` and **before** conditioning through a pure `prepare_eval_metadata(metadata, rotation_plan, generator, stream, canon_stream)`; `fa_invariant` + `--canonicalize` → `ValueError`.
    70	  - **Protocol binding, fail-closed (review B2):** `resolve_canonicalize_protocol(flag, file_cfg, embedded_cfg, allow_mismatch)` runs BEFORE model construction. Whenever canonicalization is *in play* (trained on per the file config, applied by the flag, or `--allow-canon-mismatch` given): the checkpoint MUST carry `model_config` (absence fatal — all three comparator checkpoints do; `train.py`'s `ModelConfigEmbedderCallback` writes it); the embedded and file `training.canonicalize` blocks are compared **type-strictly** (absent ≠ `{enabled:false}` ≠ `{enabled:true}`; `1` ≠ `true`); disagreement is fatal and is NOT what `--allow-canon-mismatch` authorizes — that flag only permits `trained ≠ applied` when embedded == file. Record fields: `canonicalize_trained`, `canonicalize_applied`, `canonicalize_mismatch`, `canonicalize_img_w`, `canonicalize_tiebreak`, `canonicalize_tie_counts` (dict), `model_config_sha256_file`, `model_config_sha256_embedded`. Filenames: `_canon` when applied, `_canonmis` when a mismatch was authorized (so trained-but-not-applied cells are also distinguishable), placed after the method suffix and before the rotation token; absent flags ⇒ names and records **byte-identical** to today (tested on fixed inputs; injectivity tested across canonical/non-canonical × mismatch × fixed/random × vanilla/FA × ARE for both metrics and predictions names).
    71	  - **Canonical sidecar** `<metrics>.canon.json` (written whenever `--canonicalize`): per position `(i, target_id, dj, tie_class, canonical_depth_sha256)` where the hash is over `D_canon.contiguous().numpy().tobytes()` (float64, bitwise-stable ⇒ equal across rotations), plus `canon_hash` = sha256 of the ordered tuple list and `tie_counts`. The exp_14 `.stream.json` contract is untouched.
    72	  - **Scored-waveform dump (announcement 08; review B3):** `--dump-scored-waveforms <dir>` writes, per cell, one `.npy` `[N, 10240]` float32 of the **post-decode, post-clamp, post-pad** tensors — exactly the tensor passed to `metric_callback.update_metrics` (a test spies on the call) — in stream order, with `<stem>.sha256` and a manifest `{n, dtype, shape, sha256, stream input_hash}`. The existing `--store_predictions` (pre-clamp) is left unchanged.
    73	
